      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.06070_p3_b1",
      "text": "Human Preference Alignment Preference optimization was initially developed for large language\nmodels (LLMs) to align the outputs of pre-trained models with human preferences [7, 33]. In the\ntraditional RLHF paradigm, human preferences are first used to train a reward model, and the target\nmodel is then optimized via reinforcement learning by maximizing the reward scores [29]. However,\nthis pipeline has been widely criticized for its inefficiency. Fortunately, DPO [26] provides a more\nefficient alternative by eliminating the need for a reward model and relying solely on offline preference\ndataset, and has thus been widely studied [1, 41].\n",
      "bbox": [
        108.0,
        123.44,
        505.24,
        198.94
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        432,
        493,
        2020,
        795
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.95,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Medi",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 645,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.06070_p3_b2",
      "text": "Alignment of Diffusion Models Preference alignment for diffusion models has recently attracted\nincreasing attention [38, 14]. Most existing methods are built upon the DPO paradigm. For instance,\nDiffusion-DPO [35] reformulates likelihood optimization as error modeling, resulting in a tractable\ntraining objective. DSPO [42] leverages score matching to align the post-training objective with\nthe pre-training objective. DMPO [16] performs preference alignment by optimizing the reverse\nKL divergence. MaPO [11] directly optimizes the likelihood margin between image pairs, yielding\na reference-free approach. Despite its efficiency advantages, DPO is limited by discrete binary\npreferences and cannot benefit from the rich reward signals in traditional RLHF, thus leading to\nsuboptimal optimization. In this work, we introduce fine-grained preferences extracted from Arena\ninto DPO, enabling both efficient and more effective alignment.\n",
      "bbox": [
        107.64,
        205.23,
        505.25,
        313.51
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        430,
